WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:05.060
Thanks to Venom: Let There Be Carnage, a whole
new generation of fans have been introduced

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:07.169
to the Marvel villain Shriek.

00:00:07.169 --> 00:00:10.950
But who is this elusive criminal, and just
what makes her tick?

00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:14.339
This is Shriek's story in the Marvel Universe.

00:00:14.339 --> 00:00:20.020
It may seem obvious, but it's worth emphasizing
nonetheless: Shriek's association with Carnage

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:21.130
is indelible.

00:00:21.130 --> 00:00:24.570
In fact, she rarely shows up in the comics
without him.

00:00:24.570 --> 00:00:29.240
The character was created by writer and editor
Tom DeFalco and introduced in Spider-Man Unlimited

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:34.360
#1, the first installment of the sprawling
"Maximum Carnage" storyline.

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:40.050
In fact, it's Carnage himself who first introduces
us to Shriek, during his escape from the Ravencroft

00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:42.030
Institute for the Criminally In sane.

00:00:42.030 --> 00:00:46.960
As he is slaughtering guards and orderlies,
Cletus hears another Ravencroft inmate cheering

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:47.990
him on.

00:00:47.990 --> 00:00:53.579
Curious, he discovers Shriek in a nearby cell,
wrapped in a straight jacket and very excited

00:00:53.579 --> 00:00:55.670
about his on-going horror show.

00:00:55.670 --> 00:01:00.579
She convinces Carnage to free her, and the
two of them engage in a twisted romance and

00:01:00.579 --> 00:01:05.400
a campaign of chaos that stretches across
14 more issues.

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:09.440
While there have been occasional attempts
to utilize Shriek as a character in her own

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:11.850
right, none of them have ever stuck.

00:01:11.850 --> 00:01:17.690
From her first appearance in 1993 to her apparent
death in 2019, she has been almost entirely

00:01:17.690 --> 00:01:23.030
defined as Carnage's love interest, inevitably
turning up whenever he does.

00:01:23.030 --> 00:01:27.450
So maybe it's no wonder she turns up in Let
There Be Carnage — after all, wherever Cletus

00:01:27.450 --> 00:01:31.000
Kasady goes, Shriek inevitably follows.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:35.690
"There's gonna be carnage."

00:01:35.690 --> 00:01:40.050
The movie version of Shriek will likely be
different from the comic book version in a

00:01:40.050 --> 00:01:44.630
number of ways, but the biggest involves how
she actually gets her superpowers.

00:01:44.630 --> 00:01:48.970
In the comics, Shriek is supposedly a mutant
— or at least that's what her rendition

00:01:48.970 --> 00:01:51.670
of an origin story would seem to indicate.

00:01:51.670 --> 00:01:56.470
The exact events have never actually been
shown, but according to Shriek herself, she

00:01:56.470 --> 00:02:00.580
was a drug dealer whose powers were awakened
by the hero known as Cloak.

00:02:00.580 --> 00:02:05.070
Famously, Cloak's body acts as a portal to
an alternate plane of existence called the

00:02:05.070 --> 00:02:07.000
Darkforce Dimension.

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:12.739
Being enveloped in Cloak's cloak often results
in criminals experiencing terrifying visions

00:02:12.739 --> 00:02:17.060
of their greatest fears, and can lead to them
losing their minds.

00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:21.999
That's exactly what happened to Shriek, too
— only in her case, the loss of her sanity

00:02:21.999 --> 00:02:25.349
coincided with the emergence of her newfound
abilities.

00:02:25.349 --> 00:02:29.840
Don't expect to hear about any of this in
the movie, though, as both Cloak and mutantkind

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:34.049
in general are currently the property of Marvel
Studios.

00:02:34.049 --> 00:02:36.450
Speaking of Shriek's superpowers...

00:02:36.450 --> 00:02:37.450
What are they?

00:02:37.450 --> 00:02:41.180
You'd probably guess it has something to do
with her voice, and you'd be right.

00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:45.889
Shriek's primary power is her ability to manipulate
sound, which usually takes the form of blasts

00:02:45.889 --> 00:02:47.239
of sonic energy.

00:02:47.239 --> 00:02:51.939
As you might expect, this is bad news for
symbiotes, who are vulnerable to sound, but

00:02:51.939 --> 00:02:56.909
the concussive force of Shriek's attacks can
do extreme physical damage to normal people

00:02:56.909 --> 00:02:58.120
as well.

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:02.829
Despite being her signature power, in the
comics, Shriek's sonic abilities are actually

00:03:02.829 --> 00:03:05.540
the least terrifying weapon in her arsenal.

00:03:05.540 --> 00:03:11.090
Beyond sound manipulation, Shriek also seems
to have an undefined degree of psychic power.

00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:16.480
She has demonstrated limited telekinesis by
levitating people and objects, but more interestingly,

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:22.159
she can reach into the minds of others, locating
and drawing out the darkest parts of their

00:03:22.159 --> 00:03:23.159
personalities.

00:03:23.159 --> 00:03:27.590
Shriek has used this ability on Spider-Man
in an attempt to turn him to the dark side,

00:03:27.590 --> 00:03:32.499
and on the Ravencroft inmate Malcom McBride
as well, re-awakening his villainous alter

00:03:32.499 --> 00:03:33.970
ego, Carrion.

00:03:33.970 --> 00:03:38.700
Over the course of "Maximum Carnage," her
powers even affect the entire city of New

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:44.669
York, plunging it into a chaos driven by humanity's
darkest impulses.

00:03:44.669 --> 00:03:49.540
At first glance, Shriek may seem like your
run-of-the-mill psychopath, but there's actually

00:03:49.540 --> 00:03:51.449
a lot more to her than that.

00:03:51.449 --> 00:03:55.939
While she didn't do a whole lot in the wake
of "Maximum Carnage," the character was fleshed

00:03:55.939 --> 00:04:00.420
out in a four-issue Spider-Man story called
"Shrieking," which played out over the course

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:03.729
of Amazing Spider-Man #390-393.

00:04:03.729 --> 00:04:09.390
DeMatteis' tale digs deeper into Shriek's
most interesting character trait: her desire

00:04:09.390 --> 00:04:10.980
for a family.

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:16.180
"Maximum Carnage" had already introduced this
idea, with Shriek and Carnage serving as twisted

00:04:16.180 --> 00:04:22.290
parental figures for fellow villains Doppelganger,
Demogoblin, and Carrion, but "Shrieking" takes

00:04:22.290 --> 00:04:23.950
it much further.

00:04:23.950 --> 00:04:28.290
Here, Shriek essentially kidnaps Malcolm McBride
from Ravencroft.

00:04:28.290 --> 00:04:32.950
Shriek still considers Carrion her son, and
moreover, she's convinced McBride's actual

00:04:32.950 --> 00:04:34.840
mother doesn't really love him.

00:04:34.840 --> 00:04:39.030
As it turns out, this is because Shriek herself
did not have loving parents.

00:04:39.030 --> 00:04:44.010
Her childhood was filled with a wide variety
of abuse, and the trauma she endured at her

00:04:44.010 --> 00:04:47.470
family's hands left deep scars on her psyche.

00:04:47.470 --> 00:04:51.920
Her need to be a mother to Carrion is rooted
in her desire to embody the kind of mother

00:04:51.920 --> 00:04:56.980
she never had — which is why, when she finally
realizes that McBride's mother does actually

00:04:56.980 --> 00:04:59.010
love him, she lets him go.

00:04:59.010 --> 00:05:03.110
It's a surprisingly moving and compelling
story; one made even more impactful considering

00:05:03.110 --> 00:05:08.350
Shriek would essentially be absent from comics
for the next 15 years.

00:05:08.350 --> 00:05:13.220
After "Shrieking," Shriek's status as a tragic
villain was already pretty well established,

00:05:13.220 --> 00:05:15.800
but the real tragedy was yet to come.

00:05:15.800 --> 00:05:21.140
Minus one or two extremely minor appearances,
Shriek didn't appear in Marvel Comics from

00:05:21.140 --> 00:05:23.430
1995 to 2010.

00:05:23.430 --> 00:05:27.520
She was then brought back for a five-issue
Carnage mini-series starring Spider-Man and

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:32.140
Iron Man, which begins with Shriek having
made some real progress in her therapy thanks

00:05:32.140 --> 00:05:33.700
to Dr. Tanis Nieves.

00:05:33.700 --> 00:05:38.160
However, when the ruthless corporation Hall
Industries makes her a pawn in a scheme to

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:43.660
weaponize the Carnage symbiote, Shriek ends
up relapsing into her violent ways and returning

00:05:43.660 --> 00:05:45.320
to Carnage's side.

00:05:45.320 --> 00:05:49.850
But then Nieves, who by then has merged with
a symbiote of her own to become Scorn, turns

00:05:49.850 --> 00:05:54.160
on Shriek, comparing her to a dog who needs
to be put down.

00:05:54.160 --> 00:05:56.940
Before long, Shriek has wound up in a coma.

00:05:56.940 --> 00:06:01.180
After that, Shriek's main appearances usually
involve her coming back to Carnage again and

00:06:01.180 --> 00:06:04.540
again, no matter how abusive he is towards
her.

00:06:04.540 --> 00:06:10.820
Finally, in Absolute Carnage: Lethal Protectors
#1, she willingly sacrifices herself to Carnage's

00:06:10.820 --> 00:06:14.930
newfound religious cause, allowing him to
rip out her spine.

00:06:14.930 --> 00:06:20.810
All things considered, it's not the kindest
of endings for poor ol' Shriek.

00:06:20.810 --> 00:06:25.750
Of course, no one is dead forever in comic
books, and despite the traumatic end to her

00:06:25.750 --> 00:06:29.920
story, there's actually a very clear path
set for Shriek's eventual return.

00:06:29.920 --> 00:06:32.760
In fact, you could argue that she's returned
already.

00:06:32.760 --> 00:06:37.220
After Shriek's sacrifice in Absolute Carnage,
Carnage himself resurrected the character

00:06:37.220 --> 00:06:42.870
known as Demogoblin, an old Spider-Man foe
who comes from the infernal realm of limbo.

00:06:42.870 --> 00:06:46.060
To do this, Shriek's body was offered up as
a vessel.

00:06:46.060 --> 00:06:50.680
Having possessed Shriek's body, the demon
began presenting as female, calling itself

00:06:50.680 --> 00:06:51.930
Demagoblin.

00:06:51.930 --> 00:06:57.300
Between Carnage referring to Demagoblin as
being "merged with Shriek," and the two immediately

00:06:57.300 --> 00:07:02.760
engaging in a romantic relationship, it seems
logical that Shriek is still in there somewhere.

00:07:02.760 --> 00:07:07.410
And it's worth knowing that Demagoblin has
a history of ripping free of its host body,

00:07:07.410 --> 00:07:10.660
restoring the host's original form in the
process.

00:07:10.660 --> 00:07:14.800
Whether or not we'll ever find out for sure
if Shriek is still in there depends on what

00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:16.120
happens with Demagoblin.

00:07:16.120 --> 00:07:20.820
After Absolute Carnage, she appeared as a
last-page reveal in Scream: Curse of Carnage

00:07:20.820 --> 00:07:25.630
#6, but that turned out to be the last issue,
as the book was delayed due to the COVID-19

00:07:25.630 --> 00:07:28.400
pandemic and subsequently canceled.

00:07:28.400 --> 00:07:33.430
Shriek fans can only hope that her debut in
Let There Be Carnage might make her popular

00:07:33.430 --> 00:07:38.010
enough to one day warrant a full return in
the comics.

00:07:38.010 --> 00:07:43.100
Just because Shriek is primarily associated
with Carnage, doesn't mean she has only appeared

00:07:43.100 --> 00:07:44.690
in Carnage's comics.

00:07:44.690 --> 00:07:49.450
Aside from "Shrieking," the one story that
actually centers around her, she has had scattered

00:07:49.450 --> 00:07:51.960
cameos in a wide variety of books.

00:07:51.960 --> 00:07:56.210
Following the Carnage miniseries, she was
one of Caroline Le Fay's Doom Maidens in the

00:07:56.210 --> 00:08:01.760
2013 series Fearless Defenders before becoming
a member of the Sinister Sixteen in the pages

00:08:01.760 --> 00:08:03.750
of Superior Foes of Spider-Man.

00:08:03.750 --> 00:08:09.460
Both series were excellent, but Shriek didn't
get much of a chance to shine in them, as

00:08:09.460 --> 00:08:13.560
in each instance she was just one part of
a much larger team.

00:08:13.560 --> 00:08:18.850
In 2016, she briefly appeared in Spider-Woman
#1, and later got a slightly larger role in

00:08:18.850 --> 00:08:23.050
the first couple of issues of Great Lakes
Avengers, in which she can be seen working

00:08:23.050 --> 00:08:25.960
as an enforcer for Detroit villain Nain Rouge.

00:08:25.960 --> 00:08:31.560
In 2018, Shriek finally came full circle,
returning to symbiote territory by attacking

00:08:31.560 --> 00:08:38.749
Venom in Venom #157, before reuniting with
Carnage one last time in the Absolute Carnage

00:08:38.749 --> 00:08:40.689
storyline.

00:08:40.689 --> 00:08:46.509
The first Venom movie wasn't exactly what
you'd call a prestige film.

00:08:46.509 --> 00:08:53.199
Eddie catching and eating the lobster

00:08:53.199 --> 00:08:56.249
And the sequel appears to be carrying on in
the same bizarre tone.

00:08:56.249 --> 00:09:00.579
That said, it's interesting that the movie
stars English actor Naomie Harris as Shriek,

00:09:00.579 --> 00:09:04.319
who is famously selective about the roles
she takes.

00:09:04.319 --> 00:09:09.439
Harris burst onto the cinema scene in 2002
as Selena in the zombie movie 28 Days Later,

00:09:09.439 --> 00:09:13.629
for which she won the Black Reel Award for
Outstanding Breakthrough Performance.

00:09:13.629 --> 00:09:18.480
In the two ensuing decades, she has appeared
in some blockbuster franchises too, including

00:09:18.480 --> 00:09:22.259
the Pirates of the Caribbean movies and the
James Bond franchise.

00:09:22.259 --> 00:09:27.990
In 2016, Harris earned an Academy Award nomination
for her portrayal of Paula, the lead character's

00:09:27.990 --> 00:09:30.350
abusive mother in Moonlight.

00:09:30.350 --> 00:09:35.699
Considering Harris' obvious talent as a performer,
her portrayal of Shriek could be a very special

00:09:35.699 --> 00:09:37.089
one indeed.

00:09:37.089 --> 00:09:42.730
If nothing else, she might just make a household
name of a character deserving of more attention

00:09:42.730 --> 00:09:45.089
than she has previously received.

